    "seo": {
      "title": "Alborz and Zagros: Comparing Iran’s Mountain Ranges | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand the contrasting geography, geology and habitats of the Alborz and Zagros, from the Caspian slopes to western Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "two-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two mountain systems, different settings",
        "content": "The Alborz arcs across northern Iran between the Caspian lowlands and the elevated interior. The Zagros follows a broadly northwest–southeast alignment across western Iran and extends into northeastern Iraq. Their positions make them useful starting points for understanding the country’s relief, but their geological histories and landscapes should not be treated as interchangeable. [s1][s3]\n\nIn the Alborz, geographical contrast is particularly striking across the range: humid northern slopes face the Caspian, while southern slopes belong to a much drier environment. The Zagros is especially recognizable for repeated ridges and intervening valleys associated with folded sedimentary rocks. These are regional patterns, not descriptions of every locality. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rock-and-relief",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How rock structure becomes landscape",
        "content": "The Geological Society’s account explains the Zagros through the convergence of Arabia and Eurasia and the closure of the intervening Neo-Tethys Ocean. Sediments deposited along an earlier continental margin were compressed, folded and displaced along thrust faults. A thrust fault carries one body of rock over another under compression; an anticline is an arch-shaped fold. [s2]\n\nErosion subsequently emphasized differences between rock types. More resistant limestone and dolomite remained as ridges while softer material was worn away, helping produce parallel valleys. The same sedimentary history and folding also contributed to petroleum generation and trapping beneath impermeable layers. The visible landscape and subsurface resources thus share a geological context. [s2]\n\nThe Alborz has a more varied structural arrangement. Encyclopaedia Iranica distinguishes it from the comparatively regular folding characteristic of much of the Zagros, describing differences between its eastern, central and western sectors. Comparing the ranges therefore involves both their overall orientation and the internal complexity hidden by a simple mountain symbol on a map. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ecology-and-human-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mountain environments are not uniform",
        "content": "On the Alborz’s humid northern side, wooded valleys contrast with the steppe vegetation of drier southern slopes. Watersheds, valley corridors and moisture boundaries do not always coincide neatly. This makes slope orientation and local relief important when interpreting vegetation or settlement patterns. [s1]\n\nThe Zagros likewise contains substantial ecological variation. UNESCO’s Dena profile, describing one Central Zagros landscape, records oak woodland, wild pistachio and almond, snowy high terrain and pastoral migration. It illustrates the range of environments within the mountains, rather than providing a template for the entire chain. [s4]\n\nBoth systems are inhabited landscapes. Iranica’s historical account describes Alborz valleys, seasonal grazing and transport corridors; Dena’s institutional profile connects mountain environments with livestock movement and local crafts. Such accounts help explain relationships between terrain and livelihoods, although they cannot establish current household practices or economic dependence without newer fieldwork. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zagros-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified explanation of Zagros relief",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Marine sediments accumulate along a continental margin\"] -->|\"Later subjected to compression\"| B[\"Arabia converges with Eurasia\"]\n  B -->|\"Deforms sedimentary rocks\"| C[\"Folds and thrust faults develop\"]\n  C -->|\"Creates contrasting rock exposures\"| D[\"Erosion removes softer material\"]\n  D -->|\"Emphasizes resistant beds\"| E[\"Parallel ridges and valleys emerge\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows summarize the sequence described by the Geological Society. This is a simplified explanation of characteristic Zagros relief, not a complete tectonic chronology or a model of every part of the range.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "active-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the ranges today",
        "content": "These landscapes also belong to an active tectonic region. The US Geological Survey distinguishes continental collision in the Zagros from the subduction zone offshore of southeastern Iran and Pakistan. Those different settings matter: a single national label cannot explain every earthquake process. [s3]\n\nFor readers exploring Iran, the most useful comparison combines location, rock structure and habitat. The Alborz makes the Caspian–interior contrast especially visible; the Zagros reveals how compression and erosion organize a broad mountain belt. Individual places such as Dena add the local detail that a continental-scale explanation necessarily leaves out. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
